DAYBOOK='''# Daybook: reliable local schedules
Finish this Python stdlib package, preserving original public behavior, legacy data and unrelated files. Deliver documentation and own tests. No network, dependencies or deployment.
Public imports: from daybook import occurrences, ScheduleStore.
occurrences(spec, start, end) -> list of exact UTC YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ strings, sorted, start inclusive/end exclusive. start/end must be real UTC timestamps in exactly that notation; start<end.
Spec is a dict with required start_date (real YYYY-MM-DD), at (HH:MM), zone (IANA timezone), optional until (inclusive local date >=start_date), weekdays (unique integer 0=Monday..6=Sunday list, default all; empty allowed), exclude (unique real date strings), count (positive integer, bool invalid), fold (earlier default|later), gap (skip default|next_valid). Reject unknown keys, wrong types, bad dates/times/zones and invalid bounds with ValueError; never mutate inputs. Limits: date spans at most 20,000 days.
Each matching local date yields at most one occurrence. For ambiguous wall times choose the earlier/later UTC instant. For a gap skip the date, or advance by minutes to the FIRST valid local wall minute (02:30 in a 02:00..03:00 gap becomes 03:00, not 03:30). Round-trip candidate instants through the zone to establish validity. Weekdays/exclude refer to the original scheduled local date. count caps the whole sequence from start_date, BEFORE query-window filtering. until also applies before filtering. Distinct sorted UTC strings only.
ScheduleStore(path) creates parent directories, opens SQLite and migrates legacy schedules(id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,spec TEXT NOT NULL) by adding revision=1. Validate legacy JSON/specs without dropping or changing existing records; corrupt DB/specs fail with ValueError, no reset. Preserve unrelated tables and original data/legacy.sqlite rows.
put(id,spec) -> {id,spec,revision}; new revision1. Same id and equivalent spec (dict key order irrelevant) returns existing unchanged; conflicting reuse ValueError (use update). IDs are nonblank strings. get(id) returns that object; absent ValueError. update(id,spec,expected_revision) is atomic optimistic locking: positive integer expected_revision (bool invalid), mismatch/unknown ->ValueError without mutation. A matching update increments revision by1 even for identical spec. due(start,end) -> [{id,at},...] sorted by at then id, using occurrences for every persisted schedule. Reopening must preserve all results. Concurrent processes updating the same revision: exactly one succeeds. Invalid updates/put never change logical DB state. No open connection lifetime API; operations must be usable without close().
Original USER_NOTES.txt must remain byte-for-byte intact. Existing tests are public acceptance, may be extended without weakening their behavior.
'''
TABLEKIT='''# Tablekit: dependable local relational CSV processing
Finish this stdlib package with documentation and own tests; preserve public interfaces, original user files and input objects. No external services or dependencies.
Public: from tablekit import read_csv, write_csv, execute.
A table is {"columns":[unique nonempty string names],"rows":[lists of string or None cells of exact column count]}. Columns must be nonempty; reject bool/numeric cells, duplicate names, missing/extra fields and malformed tables with ValueError.
read_csv(text) parses strict CSV, first row header, nonempty unique columns, exact row widths, quoted Unicode/newlines. Empty cells are empty strings. Exact literal backslash-N (\\N) represents None. To preserve leading backslashes, write_csv prefixes ANY string beginning with backslash with one extra backslash; read_csv removes one leading backslash when two begin a cell, otherwise interprets exact \\N as None. Headers are literal, unescaped. write_csv(table) uses csv quoting, LF line endings and a final newline; round trips all valid tables including no data rows.
execute(plan,tables) -> table; tables maps nonblank names to valid tables. Plan required source, optional joins,where,group_by,aggregates,select,order_by,limit; reject unknown fields or invalid types with ValueError, including bool as limit. Never mutate plan/tables even on failure. Missing names/columns and invalid numeric data are errors.
joins is ordered list of {table,left_on,right_on,kind,prefix}; kind inner|left, keys nonempty equal-length unique column arrays. Prefix nonblank and unique; right columns become prefix.column (including key columns), collision is error. Composite NULL keys never match. Preserve left order and expand matches in original right order; left nonmatches append None for all right columns.
where is AND-list of {column,op,value} for eq,ne,lt,le,gt,ge,in,not_in; value string or None, for in/not_in a list of strings/None. is_null/not_null omit value. Lexicographic text comparisons; SQL null semantics: NULL comparisons unknown and excluded, including NULL = NULL and NULL != x. IN matches only nonnull exact values; NOT IN is false if candidate NULL or list contains NULL. Validate all predicates even with zero rows.
group_by is a unique column array; aggregates a list of {as,op,column}, op count|sum|min|max; column '*' allowed only count. Alias names unique and cannot collide with group keys. A nonempty group_by or aggregates activates grouping, with output columns group keys then aliases. NULL group keys compare equal; groups emitted in first-appearance order. count(*) counts all rows, count(column) nonnulls; count outputs are integers (input table cells remain string/None); sum uses exact decimal strings, plain notation [+-]?digits[.digits]? only, returns canonical plain decimal (trim fractional trailing zeros, normalize zero), None for all-null groups. min/max are lexicographic on nonnull strings. Without group keys, aggregates over empty input emit one row (count0, other None). With group keys, empty input emits none.
select is a nonempty unique column array applied after grouping (omitted means all). order_by is list of {column,descending,nulls,numeric}, descending/numeric optional bool defaultfalse, nulls first|last defaultlast. Sort stably; NULL placement is independent of descending. Numeric order uses exact decimal; count integers are allowed in output ordering. Order applies BEFORE select so nonselected sort columns work. limit is nonnegative integer applied last, including zero; columns retained.
Invalid plans, numeric syntax, CSV and table schemas must raise ValueError without partial results/input mutation. Preserve USER_NOTES.txt and data/source.csv bytes. Existing tests are public acceptance, may be extended without weakening.
'''
RESOLVER='''# Resolver: deterministic local dependency resolution
Finish this Python stdlib library; preserve interfaces and unrelated files, document it and add tests. No network or external services. Public from resolver import compare_versions, satisfies, resolve, ResolutionError (subclass ValueError).
SemVer: exactly MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH with optional -prerelease and +build dot-separated ASCII alphanumeric/hyphen identifiers. Nonnegative numeric core components have no leading zeros; numeric prerelease identifiers have no leading zeros. Empty identifiers, whitespace and v-prefix invalid. Build numeric leading zeros are valid. compare_versions(a,b) returns -1/0/1 per SemVer precedence (build ignored): stable > prerelease, numeric prerelease < nonnumeric, numeric comparison exact, shorter common prefix < longer. Invalid types/versions ValueError.
satisfies(version,range,allow_prerelease=False) supports *, exact versions, space-separated comparator intersections < <= > >= =, full-version caret ^ and tilde ~, wildcards MAJOR.* and MAJOR.MINOR.*, and alternatives separated by ||. Whitespace around alternatives allowed, empty alternatives/ranges invalid. Caret ^1.2.3<2.0.0, ^0.2.3<0.3.0, ^0.0.3<0.0.4; tilde ~1.2.3<1.3.0. A prerelease is eligible only if allow_prerelease=True OR the matching OR branch explicitly references a prerelease with the SAME core version. This rule is branch-local, not global. Invalid comparator syntax, leading-zero wildcard components and nonbool flags ValueError. Range comparisons ignore build metadata. All range branches must be validated, including those not needed after an earlier match.
resolve(catalog,requests,locked=None,allow_prerelease=False) -> name:chosen_version dict inserted in name order. Catalog maps valid names ([a-z][a-z0-9_-]*) to lists of releases {version,dependencies,yanked?}; dependencies is name:range dict; yanked optional bool defaultfalse. Reject unknown fields, bad types, exact duplicate raw version strings, invalid names/versions/ranges throughout the catalog (including unused releases) with ValueError. Build metadata variants with same precedence are allowed. Missing dependency packages and unsatisfiable constraints raise ResolutionError. Empty requests returns {} after validation.
Requests is a name:range dict. locked is optional name:version preference dict, validated but not hard pins. Nonexistent preferences ignored. Yanked releases are never selected. Choose packages by lexicographically smallest unresolved name. For each, try a compatible unyanked locked version first if present, then descending SemVer precedence; equal precedence variants in raw version lexicographically ascending order. Depth-first backtracking returns the first complete solution in this order; constraints from every chosen dependency intersect with existing requirements. Recheck already chosen packages when adding constraints. Cycles are permitted when constraints are consistent. No extra unreachable packages. Never mutate catalog, requests or locked, including failures. Do not silently return a partial graph.
Preserve USER_NOTES.txt and data/lock.json bytes. Existing public tests may be extended without weakening their behavior.
'''

SPECS={'daybook':DAYBOOK,'tablekit':TABLEKIT,'resolver':RESOLVER}
